@page "/HowItWorks/net-proceeds"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/net-proceeds">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Fees go on the trade they belong to</h2>
            <p>
                Dealing costs are allowable, whether they are paid on a purchase or on a sale. The app puts them on the side
                of the trade they belong to, so that every trade carries a single net figure:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Acquisitions:</strong> cost = price paid <strong>+</strong> commission, stamp duty and other fees.</li>
                <li><strong>Disposals:</strong> proceeds = amount received <strong>−</strong> commission and other fees, i.e. <strong>net proceeds</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Where the fees come from depends on the broker. For Interactive Brokers statements, commission and taxes
                are read separately and converted to pounds at the exchange rate given in the statement. For FreeTrade,
                Trading 212 and Interactive Investor exports, the app uses the broker's total amount in pounds, which already
                includes the dealing charges. Some trades have later adjustments, such as an option premium that is added
                to the shares it was exercised into. Those adjustments are applied to the same net figure.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Fees are split by quantity</h2>
            <p>
                When a trade is split across matches, its fees are split too, in proportion to quantity. Matches use the
                trade's net figure × (shares matched ÷ total shares).
            </p>
            <p>
                In the <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">same-day example</a>, two sells on one day
                carry £5 of fees each. They are combined into one disposal of 80 shares with £10 of fees. 50 shares are
                matched same day and 30 with the Section 104 pool, so the fees are split:
            </p>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Match</th><th class="text-end">Shares</th><th class="text-end">Share of selling fees</th><th class="text-end">Net proceeds</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Same day</td><td class="text-end">50</td><td class="text-end">£10 × 50/80 = £6.25</td><td class="text-end">£750 − £6.25 = £743.75</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Section 104</td><td class="text-end">30</td><td class="text-end">£10 × 30/80 = £3.75</td><td class="text-end">£450 − £3.75 = £446.25</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Because a day's trades are combined first, fees from all of that day's sells are added together before they
                are split. You cannot assign a fee to one particular match.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">What the yearly summary shows</h2>
            <p>
                The <strong>Disposal Proceeds</strong> in the CGT Yearly Summary are <em>net</em> of selling fees, and the
                <strong>Allowable Costs</strong> do <em>not</em> include selling fees again. The tax return only accepts whole
                pounds, so the summary rounds in your favour: proceeds and gains down, allowable costs and losses up.
            </p>
            <p>
                HMRC's computation working sheet in the
                <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/self-assessment-capital-gains-summary-sa108" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SA108 notes</a>
                works the same way. It subtracts the incidental costs of disposal from the proceeds to give "net disposal
                proceeds", and the total costs include only the cost of acquisition.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Comparing with a calculator that uses gross proceeds</h2>
            <p>
                Some calculators and guides report gross proceeds instead and add the selling fees to allowable costs, for
                example <a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/how-to-calculate-capital-gains-tax-on-shares/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser</a>.
                The gain is the same either way. Only the split between "proceeds" and "costs" moves by the amount of
                the selling fees:
            </p>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Same-day example</th><th class="text-end">Proceeds</th><th class="text-end">Allowable costs</th><th class="text-end">Gain</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>This app (net proceeds)</td><td class="text-end">£1,190</td><td class="text-end">£1,010</td><td class="text-end">£180</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Gross proceeds, selling fees in costs</td><td class="text-end">£1,200</td><td class="text-end">£1,020</td><td class="text-end">£180</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                If you compare this app with a calculator that uses gross proceeds, expect the proceeds and costs to differ
                by the total selling fees while the gains agree. If you would rather enter gross proceeds on your return,
                add the selling fees back to both figures. The Imported Trades page shows the gross and net amount of each
                trade.
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/how-to-calculate-capital-gains-tax-on-shares/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser: how to calculate CGT on shares</a> (independent site)</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/self-assessment-capital-gains-summary-sa108" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC SA108 Capital Gains summary and notes</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg15250" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG15250</a>: incidental costs of acquisition and disposal</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
